For my understanding: this only works with starting the browser in ui-mode (so non-headless), correct? While the injected script/this PR implementation could run on a headless one, right? 

We were hoping to not really have to run the browser with UI during test generation/discovery, but we will see if that works for us, I will leave this open for now and comment back later if it works.


Re your question: we want to control the browser during test discovery/generation and predict elements we will interact with, that means of course we have the full DOM and elements in-memory and can relatively easily come up with "some" selector that works ok on our own (currently by just injecting test-id attributes) but want the "best"/"most reliable" selector for the generated test case in the end -> we would like to use playwright code for that since rolling our own "selector hierachy" will likely be worse and get outdated from upstream than what you do.

It also works in headless env, see e.g. this:

```ts
import { chromium } from 'playwright';

(async () => {
  // Setup
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  const context = await browser.newContext();
  const page = await context.newPage();

  await page.setContent(
      '<html lang="en"><body><button id="button">Some Text</button></body></html>'
  );
  const locator = await page.locator('#button').evaluate(e => {
    return playwright.generateLocator(e);
  });
  console.log(locator);

  // Teardown
  await context.close();
  await browser.close();
})();
```

