Here is an example.

```js
const browser = await chromium.launch();
const context = await browser.newContext();
const page = await context.newPage();
await page.goto('http://example.com')

// find the element some other way
const h1 = await page.locator("xpath=//h1");
const handle = await h1.elementHandle();

// get the generated locator from that element
const generatedLocator = await page.evaluate((handle) => {
  return {
    selector: playwright.selector(handle),
    locator: playwright.generateLocator(handle),
  };
}, handle);

console.log(generatedLocator);
```

To get this, you will need to enable the console, here is how to do that.

```
PWDEBUG=console node index.mjs
```

See PR #23921 .

To use the fork (with the subject-to-change API) already:

* `npm add https://github.com/OctoMind-dev/playwright.git`
* You will need to add a postinstall script to build playwright, e.g. `"postinstall": "cd node_modules/playwright-internal && npm ci && npm run build"`

then the api is usable in the following way:

```ts
import playwright from "playwright-internal/packages/playwright-core";

const browser = await playwright.chromium.launch();

const page = await browser.newPage();
// or of course await page.goto("https://someUrl.com");
await page.setContent(
  '<html lang="en"><body><button id="button">Some Text</button></body></html>'
);

console.log(await page.pickBestLocator(`#button`));
```

which will give you ```{
  selector: 'internal:role=button[name="Some Text"i]',
  locator: "getByRole('button', { name: 'Some Text' })"
}```
